ldm - the LTSP Display Manager
==============================

ldm shows the login screen on a thin client, authenticates the user, and
hands the session to a remote server. It is not an X display manager in the
XDMCP sense: authentication goes over SSH, and the session itself runs
wherever the chosen backend puts it.

This is the fork used by ltsp6 (https://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6): ported to
GTK3, with an xfreerdp backend, for Debian 13.


What it is made of
------------------

    src/                   ldm itself - the process that authenticates and
                           starts the session
    src/plugins/ssh/       the SSH backend: session over an SSH tunnel
    src/plugins/xfreerdp/  the RDP backend, including pre-authentication
    gtkgreet/              the greeter, a separate process ldm talks to
                           over a pipe
    themes/ltsp/           the default theme: background, logo, icons, CSS
    po/                    translations, some forty languages


How a login goes
----------------

ldm starts the greeter and asks it for a username and a password; the
greeter draws the prompts and sends back what was typed. ldm hands both to
the backend named by LDM_SESSION.

With the xfreerdp backend and RDP_PREAUTH=ssh, the credentials are tried
over SSH before xfreerdp is started at all. That is what keeps a wrong
password inside the greeter, with a message, instead of handing the client
to the RDP server's own login window - where a user can sit and retype a
password that was already known to be wrong. It needs the server's host key
to be known under the name RDP_SERVER uses.


Configuration
-------------

Everything is environment variables, and on an LTSP client they come from
lts.conf(5), which is where each one is described. ldm(1) summarises the
ones that decide what the login screen says and how it looks:

    LDM_SESSION           which backend: xfreerdp, ssh
    RDP_SERVER            where the session runs
    RDP_PREAUTH           ssh, to check the password before connecting
    LDM_THEME             a directory under /usr/share/ldm/themes
    LDM_USERNAME_PROMPT   the prompts, as Pango markup
    LDM_PASSWORD_PROMPT
    LDM_VERIFY_MESSAGE
    LDM_CLOCK_FORMAT      strftime(3), markup allowed
    LDM_SYSLOG            log to syslog instead of /var/log/ldm.log
    LDM_LOGLEVEL          7 includes what pre-authentication asked and heard

The greeter follows LANG for its own language, and needs that locale to be
generated in the image - glibc translates nothing in the C locale, C.UTF-8
included. It says so in the log when the locale is missing, rather than
quietly coming up in English.


Themes
------

A theme is a directory under /usr/share/ldm/themes. Files are looked up in
it one at a time, and anything it does not carry comes from the ltsp theme
instead - so a theme can be two images and nothing else.

The other side of that rule is worth knowing: a theme that does carry a
greeter.css replaces the default one outright rather than adding to it.

    bg.png, logo.png     background and logo
    greeter.css          GTK3 CSS, loaded above the GTK theme
    preferences, host, session, language, reboot, shutdown, backend
                         the icons on the buttons and menu entries, 24x24
                         with an alpha channel

Four labels carry a name so the CSS can address them one at a time:
#ldm-prompt, #ldm-status, #ldm-host and #ldm-clock. On a dark background
image that is usually all that is needed:

    #ldm-prompt, #ldm-status, #ldm-host, #ldm-clock {
        color: #f2f2f2;
        text-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85);
    }


Building
--------

Autotools, and the Debian packaging is in debian/:

    ./autogen.sh
    ./configure --prefix=/usr --libexecdir=/usr/libexec
    make

or, for the package:

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

Built packages are published in the signed archive at
https://mabutz.github.io/ltsp6 and attached to the releases of this
repository.


